        "tree_id": "3551ce51bc6b747aa8b4730ad2e55330030d1f3d",
        "message": "feat(NET-002): binary v2 wire contract and version handshake\n\nSix frame classes on a fixed little-endian layout: intent (11 B), snapshot\n(15 B header + 24 B per entity), hash ack (13 B), hello (4 B), welcome (3 B),\nreject (4 B). Every decoder checks the exact length first: a trailing byte is a\nrejected frame, never padding, and a snapshot whose declared count disagrees\nwith its bytes is refused instead of partially read.\n\nA version outside 2..2 is answered with reject{reason:'version'} and the socket\ncloses; it is never coerced. The hash crosses the wire as u32; hashToU32 refuses\nanything that is not 8 lowercase hex chars, so a malformed hash cannot become\n00000000.\n\nv1 JSON stays exported until the game client migrates: deleting it here would\nforce a client rewrite into a netcode task and blur the evidence.\n\nMeasured, not averaged: a 64-entity snapshot is 1551 B = 46530 B/s at 30 Hz,\n3.9x over the 12 KB/s budget. That is NET-101's AoI/delta task; this codec does\nnot claim to meet the budget.\n\nGDD-NETCODE 0.3 -> 0.4 (I9): v2 frame table, appliedSeq semantics, two measured\nnumbers, two edge cases, acceptance for handshake and round-trip.\n\nAcceptance: pnpm vitest run packages/protocol 23/23, full 51/51, verify 17/17,\ntypecheck 0, core-math 0, tracer green. The blueprint acc was the broken\npnpm test --filter form; repaired, and the failing command is kept as evidence.\nAlso fixes NEEDS_DIRECTOR: ND-010's SIGNED block sat under ND-004, making the\ntoken rotation look resolved. ND-004 is OPEN. ND-011 records the TPU/T4\ndeclaration without inventing a TPU generation path.\n\nAgent: netcode-engineer\nTask: NET-002",
        "timestamp": "2026-11-18T08:26:53Z",
